WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:04.800
In the Model tree you see the structure of the model.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.200
The structure of the model can be shown in different ways and the first one is the containment

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.200
hierarchy.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.200
The containment hierarchy shows the spatial structure of the model starting from the top

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:23.600
which is the file level, going to the project, project sites, building and within the building

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.500
you have the building stories and within the stories you would have different groups of

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.960
objects and then the individual objects.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.000
So all the way from the top, the project level up to the level of the individual element

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.060
or component as the Solibri calls these things.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:45.680
Whatever you select either in the model tree or using the info tool in the 3D view will

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.520
be displayed in the info panel and in the info panel you get a lot of information about

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.640
whatever you have selected.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.460
If you would switch to a different hierarchy, in this case a component hierarchy, it works

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.440
exactly the same way.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.680
You can navigate through the hierarchy but in this case the hierarchy is grouped based

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.280
on types of components and the main categories or classes, for instance all the types of

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:20.200
doors and then individual types and underneath all the individual occurrences or instances

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.600
of that door type.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:29.040
In this case you have a "Door 18", you may have a metal door and there are four instances

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.600
of that metal door.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:38.520
Also notice that Solibri automatically assigns a basic name based on the class of the object,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:44.160
the building story and an index number.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:51.760
The third way to display the hierarchy is a so called layer view.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.160
In the layer hierarchy use the layers which are the representation layers from the IFC

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.160
file.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.800
Depending on the software in which you made the model, this group of layers might be different.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:09.280
In this case the model was grouped in walls, furniture, columns, slabs, so this layer structure

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.560
was very close to the class hierarchy but in other models or other software or if you use

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.680
a different configuration it can be totally different.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.960
The navigation is exactly the same, you see the layer, you see the different classes and

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.760
then the individual items on that layer.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.800
Finally you get a federated floor hierarchy which solubly derives from the building story

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:34.800
information.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.280
In this case we have one building file with only four floors and they are shown again

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:46.600
as a group of objects, classes and then the individual items on that group, from that

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.000
group.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.320
For now we continue to work in the containment hierarchy.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:59.200
In relation to that hierarchy everything you select will be displayed in the info panel

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.560
and the info panel dynamically displays whatever information is attached to that object.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.680
It is typically grouped in BIM data, standard or other properties, in this case only other

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.640
properties are available and favorites where you can pre-select certain properties you always

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.320
want to see.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:24.680
Let's for instance select in the viewport one of the walls in the facade.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:30.920
Here you see the BIM data which is the information that solubly collects about that object.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:35.680
Identification is a whole set of properties and attributes which identify the object.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.800
We won't go through all of them individually but you can see that this is a way of summarizing

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.880
whatever is available for that object.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.920
For instance we can see in this case the entity uses IFC wall standard case and has a wall

00:03:50.920 --> 00:04:00.160
type, it is derived from a file in the IFC2x3 format and there is no predefined type.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:10.400
You also see that the object has a name, type and a type name and it has a unique identifier.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.640
If you click on another object in the hierarchy, for instance we click on project, we would

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:15.640
get a different structure.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:22.280
In this case this entity is an IFC project, it has a name, in this case face is one of

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:29.520
the attributes available and it has other information attached to it.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.360
Back to one of the wall objects.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.040
Apart from identification we get location information where is that object positioned

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.520
in the model and Solibri collects a series of elevations, coordinates and also the hierarchy

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.280
is part of the containment or spatial hierarchy.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.320
Quantities which Solibri derives from the geometry and all types of information collected from

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.220
different places.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:00.160
Information about the materials, in this case we see that the object is set up as a material

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.400
layer set and we can find information about the different layers.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:13.260
If a layer set, if a material layer, that layer has a thickness and the material itself

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:18.640
is a material in this case with just a basic name.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.120
Profile, in this case it's a rectangular object so it's created from a profile so that object

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.440
also has profile information.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:30.840
You find other relations defined in the IFC schema and classifications which can be defined

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:38.040
either from an IFC file or within solubly and we will use them later on to further validate

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.000
and check and verify the model.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:49.320
The standard properties are defined by building smart and they depend on the object, class

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.320
or the entity.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:58.480
In this case for walls it's common to have a property set wall common which are a set

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.560
of standard defined or default defined properties for walls.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.020
Whenever you see a property set starting with the name "Pset" you should be aware that this

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.400
is a predefined property set by building smart.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.000
In this case only one property has been used, the is external property.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.680
You can see its value, in this case its value is true which means this wall is part, is

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:26.320
in contact with the exterior environment, is part of the building envelope.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:32.080
And you also see the type, the Boolean value which means it can have a value of yes or

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.920
true or false.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.560
Other properties can also be available and in this case these are not the standard properties

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.480
by buildingSMART but the properties managed within the software.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:48.120
Here we see for instance the "BaseQuantities" which is a set of quantities exported from

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:55.200
the software, component properties, in this case the model itself contains information

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.360
for instance about the embodied carbon and energy but their values are still set at zero.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:06.120
You have the quantities of the components, you will see that there is the thickness of

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:13.840
one of the components, the mass, the area, the volume, information about the material

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.480
attached to the object and then a set of property sets.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.160
In this case the properties have a certain naming convention and that depends on the

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.160
software.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.180
In these cases the model has been created in Archicad and sometimes you even see this

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:34.560
in the property names, you see different property sets and every time each property set may

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:40.360
have one or more properties, again property, value and value type.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.040
And if you are familiar with Archicad you might recognize some of these properties.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:51.440
In this case all the default properties from Archicad are also available but they don't

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:57.900
have a direct impact on the geometry, they are just there to give you the potential use

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:04.160
for collecting information, for finding something, for filtering and also the quantities which

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:10.280
are created by Archicad which is quite an extensive list.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:15.400
At the top level, at the highest level in this case the Solibri building, this is not

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.440
an IFC entity but this is the project file itself and here you can find information about

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:26.920
the metadata which is attached to in the header of the file, for instance many of the settings

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:31.480
which are used when converting the file but that depends on how the software exports your

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:32.480
IFC file.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:38.000
You see all the file name, you see a time stamp so we know that this particular file

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.640
has been exported on the 10th of November in 2021.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.280
And you can also see originally this file was created in Graphisoft Archicad version

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:53.920
2022, you see you can see that's created from a Windows system.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:09:01.440
Also, sometimes important, the version of the IFC schema is IFC2x3.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:11.160
If we return to the main 3D view and we can select all the objects, we can also take a

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.040
quick look at the other views, other tabs but they are not functional in the Anywhere

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.040
version of the software.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:22.480
Solibri Anywhere is a viewer, it's not a model checker and the checking interface, the results

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:28.080
interface are available can be shown but they are not active.

